    "notes": "Catalog identity becomes an invariant of canonical state construction and project restore. Catalog descriptors resolve a valid key automatically; non-catalog illuminated-foam omits catalogKey. Automatic wall/custom back/auto-depot descriptors therefore receive catalog identity when they correspond to current catalog products. F-013 is directly addressed by including separator modelFile in legacy identity resolution. Full-system discovery surfaces are explicitly reviewed because catalog, designState and main are broad dependency roots; listing them does not imply code edits or behavior changes in those files. No dimensions, geometry, placement arithmetic, renderer appearance, BOM classification, or non-catalog module semantics are intentionally changed."
  },
  "tests": {
    "targeted": [
      "test/catalogSingleSource.test.js",
      "test/moduleStateConstructionRegistry.test.js"
    ],
    "fullSuite": true,
    "build": true,
    "e2e": {
      "required": true,
      "targeted": [
        "e2e/f010-module-construction.spec.mjs"
      ],
      "reason": "Catalog identity is persisted state and automatic/catalog construction is browser-visible; Chromium verifies both automatic wall and real catalog-picker persistence carry canonical keys."
    }
  },
  "risk": {
    "level": "medium",
    "notes": "The change touches canonical construction and restore identity normalization. Product dimensions and placement behavior are unchanged; the risk is incorrect identity attachment or legacy repair, covered by catalog-wide and browser persistence tests."
  },
  "migration": {
    "required": false,
    "notes": "No persisted schema version changes. Legacy states are normalized opportunistically on restore: resolvable catalog products gain their canonical key and unresolved/non-catalog states omit the field instead of storing null."
  },
  "rollback": "Revert the catalog identity commits if a runtime module receives an incorrect catalogKey or any legacy project restore regression is observed."
}
